1
00:00:00,380 --> 00:00:04,900
N.I.H. Fails to Institute Health The National Institutes of Health spends ninety-six point seven percent of its budget on

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:13,080
everything except testing whether drugs work in humans. It's like a fire department that spends ninety-six point seven percent

3
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:18,240
of its budget on fire-themed calendars and three point three percent on water.

4
00:00:18,420 --> 00:00:27,220
The Allocation Scandal. Your National Institutes of Health has the word "Health" in its name and an annual budget of forty-seven

5
00:00:27,220 --> 00:00:32,720
billion dollars. fifty-five million of you die annually from disease.

6
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:39,100
You would think those two facts would be related. I spent eighty years looking for the connection.

7
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:45,220
There isn't one. Of two hundred and forty-seven billion dollars the N.I.H.

8
00:00:45,380 --> 00:00:52,160
spent over a decade, exactly three point three percent went to testing whether treatments actually work in humans.

9
00:00:52,880 --> 00:01:00,520
The other ninety-six point seven percent funded understanding disease mechanisms, translating discoveries toward (but never

10
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:09,160
into) clinical use, training scientists, and observational programs that spend billions watching disease without treating

11
00:01:09,160 --> 00:01:14,580
it, which is called "research" the way watching someone drown from a boat is called "lifeguarding.".

12
00:01:15,460 --> 00:01:19,520
That might be defensible if the testing queue were empty. It isn't.

13
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:27,880
There are roughly nine thousand five hundred compounds with established safety profiles, and ninety-nine point seven percent

14
00:01:27,880 --> 00:01:31,520
of their possible disease applications have never been tested.

15
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:39,320
Most are off-patent, which means no pharmaceutical company will fund trials for them, which means the N.I.H.

16
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:46,840
is the only entity that could, which means it is specifically choosing not to do the one thing only it can do.

17
00:01:47,420 --> 00:01:52,860
The pipeline is overflowing with candidates. The bottleneck is trials.

18
00:01:53,260 --> 00:01:59,710
The drugs are sitting there. Safe. Ready. Unpatentable. They've been waiting for decades.

19
00:02:00,420 --> 00:02:04,380
Some of them have been waiting longer than most of your scientists have been alive.

20
00:02:05,220 --> 00:02:12,660
It's like spending ninety-six point seven percent of your grocery budget on cookbooks and three point three percent on food,

21
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:19,680
then wondering why you're starving. Except the cookbooks are very prestigious and the starving people aren't on the committee

22
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:25,480
that decides whether to buy more cookbooks. On Wishonia, we had a similar institution once.

23
00:02:26,140 --> 00:02:31,800
It spent four percent of its budget on curing disease and ninety-six percent on studying disease.

24
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:37,160
We renamed it the National Institutes of Studying Health. Then we defunded it.

25
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:42,340
It took twelve minutes. The Efficiency Gap: A Tale of Two Trials.

26
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:47,860
The difference between efficient and inefficient trial design isn't theoretical.

27
00:02:48,380 --> 00:02:52,840
Humanity accidentally ran a controlled experiment on itself during COVID.

28
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:58,300
Two countries. Same disease. One spent like it was trying to cure death.

29
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:03,080
The other actually did. RECOVER Initiative (N.I.H. Approach).

30
00:03:04,060 --> 00:03:11,660
The N.I.H. spent one point six six five billion dollars (one point one five billion dollars plus five hundred and fifteen

31
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:16,260
million dollars in twenty twenty-four) over four years and counting.

32
00:03:16,900 --> 00:03:21,140
Enrolled about thirty thousand patients. Completed zero trials.

33
00:03:21,640 --> 00:03:25,780
Cost per patient: fifty-five thousand five hundred dollars.

34
00:03:26,340 --> 00:03:32,280
That's the price of a luxury S.U.V. per person, and at the end of it, nobody got a luxury S.U.V.

35
00:03:32,540 --> 00:03:39,460
and nobody got cured. They got observed. Expensively. RECOVERY Trial (U.K.

36
00:03:39,460 --> 00:03:43,920
Approach). The British spent twenty million dollars over six months.

37
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:51,820
Enrolled forty-eight thousand patients. Found multiple effective treatments, including dexamethasone, which saved over one

38
00:03:51,820 --> 00:03:57,760
million lives. Cost per patient: five hundred dollars. That's the price of a nice dinner.

39
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:26,660
The N.I.H. spent one hundred and eleven times more per patient and achieved infinitely less (dividing by zero trials completed

40
00:04:26,660 --> 00:04:31,340
produces infinity, which is fitting because that's how long patients waited).

41
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:40,480
This efficiency gap is not unique to RECOVERY. A systematic review of sixty-four pragmatic trials found a median cost of

42
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:45,400
ninety-seven dollars per patient. The problem isn't that curing disease is hard.

43
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:50,200
The N.I.H. doesn't have a capability problem. It has a shopping problem.

44
00:04:50,800 --> 00:04:58,360
It keeps buying things you need before you cure disease (infrastructure, coordination, training, observation, overhead)

45
00:04:58,840 --> 00:05:06,840
without ever getting around to the curing part. It's been buying pots and pans for fifty years and has not yet started cooking.

46
00:05:07,260 --> 00:05:15,600
The kitchen is magnificent. Nobody has eaten. The Translation Crisis: All Theory, No Medicine.

47
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:20,360
The problem is not a shortage of ideas. It is a shortage of anyone bothering to test them.

48
00:05:21,140 --> 00:05:26,280
There are over twenty thousand F.D.A.-approved drugs that are known to be safe in humans.

49
00:05:26,880 --> 00:05:33,840
There are roughly forty-seven hundred to sixty-eight hundred clinically-tested candidates gathering dust, including three

50
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:40,260
thousand four hundred and twenty-two marketed drugs that could be repurposed for diseases nobody's tried them on.

51
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:48,400
A systematic review found five hundred and seventy-three unique safe drugs proposed for repurposing in Alzheimer's alone,

52
00:05:48,900 --> 00:05:56,050
yet less than ten percent are in active trials. And the number of clinical trials available for your grandma in Saint Louis?

53
00:05:56,140 --> 00:06:02,780
Zero. You have a warehouse of safe molecules that have never been systematically tested for new uses.

54
00:06:03,340 --> 00:06:07,840
The warehouse is full. The testing facility is closed for a team-building retreat.

55
00:06:08,460 --> 00:06:14,040
The N.I.H. is, by its own metrics, a publishing house that occasionally dabbles in medicine.

56
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:18,430
It publishes two point five million funded papers annually.

57
00:06:18,600 --> 00:06:22,300
It produces approximately fifty new drug approvals annually.

58
00:06:22,780 --> 00:06:28,160
That's a conversion rate from paper to patient of zero point zero zero two percent.

59
00:06:28,680 --> 00:06:36,740
For every fifty thousand papers, one drug reaches a human. The rest become citations in other papers, which become citations

60
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:41,280
in other papers, in an infinite loop of academics reading each other's homework.

61
00:06:42,020 --> 00:06:46,960
On Wishonia, we call this a "knowledge ouroboros." It looks productive from the inside.

62
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:51,480
From the outside, it's a snake eating its own tail and calling it lunch.

63
00:06:52,180 --> 00:06:59,040
Plenty of Knowledge. No Translation. About five percent of patients have participated in clinical trials.

64
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:02,800
About forty-four point eight percent would participate if invited.

65
00:07:03,380 --> 00:07:10,710
About nine to eleven percent have ever been invited. Nearly half of all patients would join a trial if asked.

66
00:07:10,900 --> 00:07:18,820
Almost nobody asks. Your species has a room full of volunteers holding their hands up, and a system that looked at the room

67
00:07:18,820 --> 00:07:23,080
and decided to study the clipboard instead. The clipboard is very well-studied.

68
00:07:23,400 --> 00:07:27,860
It has been studied for decades. Multiple papers have been written about the clipboard.

69
00:07:28,320 --> 00:07:36,700
The volunteers are still holding their hands up. Understanding disease and curing disease are completely different activities.

70
00:07:36,820 --> 00:07:42,350
One requires microscopes. The other requires giving people medicine and seeing if they stop dying.

71
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:48,660
The N.I.H. chose microscopes. It chose them fifty years ago and has not reconsidered since.

72
00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:56,360
The Death Toll: Opportunity Cost of Misallocation. When you allocate the vast majority of a forty-seven billion dollar annual

73
00:07:56,360 --> 00:08:03,060
budget into understanding disease instead of testing cures, you get a very well-understood population of dead people.

74
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:13,740
Cost Per Q.A.L.Y. (Quality-Adjusted Life Year). The ADAPTABLE trial proved pragmatic trials cost forty-four point one times

75
00:08:13,740 --> 00:08:20,980
less per patient than traditional R.C.T.s (nine hundred and twenty-nine dollars versus forty-one thousand dollars).

76
00:08:21,520 --> 00:08:26,480
Same science. Fewer catered meetings. Forty-four point one times more patients enrolled.

77
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:31,870
Fewer conference rooms rented. The "Death Equivalent" of Budget Misallocation.

78
00:08:32,260 --> 00:08:40,380
Annual opportunity cost: approximately one hundred million Q.A.L.Y.s and approximately seven million death-equivalents per

79
00:08:40,380 --> 00:08:48,280
year. Even the most conservative estimate: approximately ten million Q.A.L.Y.s and approximately seven hundred thousand

80
00:08:48,280 --> 00:08:56,620
preventable deaths annually. Traditional clinical trials run at two point two seven percent the efficiency of pragmatic

81
00:08:56,620 --> 00:09:01,660
trials. And that's the ceiling, because it only accounts for the sliver of the N.I.H.

82
00:09:01,800 --> 00:09:08,920
budget that goes to trials at all. If your car ran at two point two seven percent efficiency, you would push it faster than

83
00:09:08,920 --> 00:09:15,660
it drives. If your heart pumped at two point two seven percent efficiency, you'd be dead before finishing this sentence.

84
00:09:16,580 --> 00:09:22,380
Your clinical trial system runs at two point two seven percent efficiency, and you gave it a raise.

85
00:09:23,040 --> 00:09:27,170
You gave it a raise and a new building. The building has a cafeteria.

86
00:09:28,180 --> 00:09:31,880
Why This Allocation Exists. This allocation is not an accident.

87
00:09:32,700 --> 00:09:39,900
Someone paid for it. A J.A.M.A. study broke down two hundred and forty-seven point three billion dollars in N.I.H.

88
00:09:40,020 --> 00:09:45,340
spending (twenty-ten to twenty-nineteen). Each of these categories funds valuable work.

89
00:09:45,780 --> 00:09:50,700
The question is whether the ratio makes sense when thousands of safe compounds sit untested.

90
00:09:51,680 --> 00:09:58,140
Eighty-four point nine percent of the budget, or two hundred and nine point nine billion dollars, goes toward basic research

91
00:09:58,140 --> 00:10:05,720
into disease mechanisms, molecular biology, and genomics, which is essential for identifying new targets, though eighty-five

92
00:10:05,720 --> 00:10:10,200
cents of every dollar is a lot of understanding and not much testing.

93
00:10:11,140 --> 00:10:17,800
Eleven point eight percent, or twenty-nine point three billion dollars, goes to applied research that translates discoveries

94
00:10:17,800 --> 00:10:22,290
toward practical use without the final step of testing them in patients.

95
00:10:22,980 --> 00:10:30,360
The Clinical and Translational Science Awards infrastructure program, or C.T.S.A., accounts for approximately one billion

96
00:10:30,360 --> 00:10:34,500
dollars per year in trial infrastructure, coordination, and training.

97
00:10:35,520 --> 00:10:42,380
Observational studies for population health data collection, such as large-scale observational cohorts costing two point

98
00:10:42,380 --> 00:10:50,040
one six billion dollars, are valuable for identifying patterns, but observation without intervention doesn't cure anyone.

99
00:10:50,800 --> 00:10:57,140
Other applied research categories include drug characterization, health services research, and epidemiology.

100
00:10:58,040 --> 00:11:04,800
Three point three percent, or eight point one billion dollars, goes to phased clinical trials, which is the only category

101
00:11:04,800 --> 00:11:09,200
that directly tests whether treatments work in humans (Phases One through Three).

102
00:11:09,940 --> 00:11:15,740
Phase One trials receive one point five billion dollars, or eighteen point five percent of the clinical trial budget.

103
00:11:16,240 --> 00:11:20,780
Phase Two trials receive three point five billion dollars, or forty-three point two percent.

104
00:11:21,340 --> 00:11:27,540
Phase Three trials receive two point six billion dollars, or thirty-two point one percent, but the N.I.H.

105
00:11:27,700 --> 00:11:35,220
covers only three point seven to four point three percent of Phase Three costs, leaving completion to industry, which only

106
00:11:35,220 --> 00:11:43,200
funds trials with patent-protected profit potential. Other or unclassified trials account for zero point five billion dollars,

107
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:50,040
or six point two percent, for cross-phase overhead, pilot studies, and trials not classified to a single phase.

108
00:11:50,620 --> 00:11:57,760
Pragmatic trials, which are eighty-two times cheaper per patient, are severely underfunded despite proven efficiency.

109
00:11:58,300 --> 00:12:05,240
Drug companies love the N.I.H. Taxpayers absorb the risk of basic research; industry patents whatever works.

110
00:12:05,880 --> 00:12:11,370
It's like if your neighbor paid for your kitchen renovation, and then you charged them rent to eat there.

111
00:12:11,740 --> 00:12:16,570
But independent trials comparing drugs head-to-head? Those are terrifying.

112
00:12:17,160 --> 00:12:22,500
They might reveal that the eighty thousand dollar drug works about as well as the four dollar generic.

113
00:12:23,240 --> 00:12:28,920
PhRMA, the pharmaceutical lobby, has consistently opposed comparative effectiveness research.

114
00:12:29,740 --> 00:12:37,780
When Congress created the Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute, or P.C.O.R.I., in twenty ten, industry successfully

115
00:12:37,800 --> 00:12:42,960
lobbied to prohibit it from making coverage recommendations based on cost-effectiveness.

116
00:12:43,580 --> 00:12:49,180
Congress literally passed a law saying "don't fund research that might lower drug prices."

117
00:12:49,740 --> 00:12:55,420
They wrote it down. In legislation. On purpose. With their names on it.

118
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:02,400
And then they went home and told their constituents they were fighting for affordable healthcare, because your species'

119
00:13:02,760 --> 00:13:08,260
capacity for doublethink is genuinely Olympian. The revolving door helps.

120
00:13:08,260 --> 00:13:11,200
N.I.H. advisory committees include industry representatives.

121
00:13:11,980 --> 00:13:17,300
Former N.I.H. officials join pharmaceutical boards. Academic researchers depend on industry grants.

122
00:13:17,960 --> 00:13:25,920
Everyone's incentives align against cheap, fast trials testing whether expensive drugs outperform generics.

123
00:13:26,340 --> 00:13:31,220
It's not a conspiracy. Conspiracies require secrecy. This is all public.

124
00:13:31,740 --> 00:13:39,700
It's just that everyone involved happens to benefit from the same terrible outcome, purely by coincidence, repeatedly, for

125
00:13:39,700 --> 00:13:45,900
decades. On Wishonia, we have a word for this. The word is "conspiracy."

126
00:13:46,400 --> 00:13:51,890
The difference between a conspiracy and a system is whether the participants need to meet in a dark room.

127
00:13:52,320 --> 00:13:57,730
These people meet in conference rooms with catering. The outcome is identical.

128
00:13:58,200 --> 00:14:02,640
The three point three percent allocation isn't a mystery. It's a business model.

129
00:14:03,320 --> 00:14:07,120
Basic research produces publications. Publications produce tenure.

130
00:14:07,700 --> 00:14:12,900
Tenure produces more grants. Nobody in this cycle needs anyone to get better.

131
00:14:13,380 --> 00:14:21,140
Actual treatments are a byproduct that occasionally happens when someone forgets to optimize for papers and accidentally

132
00:14:21,140 --> 00:14:27,690
optimizes for results instead. The Public Goods vs. Club Goods Scam.

133
00:14:27,840 --> 00:14:32,080
The N.I.H. claims to produce "public goods" like open science and training.

134
00:14:32,460 --> 00:14:39,060
What it actually produces is a free R. and D. department for pharmaceutical companies, funded by the same people who then

135
00:14:39,060 --> 00:14:46,740
pay full price for the drugs. Taxpayers funded research behind ninety-nine percent of new drugs (three hundred and fifty-six

136
00:14:46,740 --> 00:14:51,240
drugs, twenty ten to twenty nineteen). Industry patents the winners.

137
00:14:51,640 --> 00:14:58,850
The public pays again through high drug prices. When research leads to a dead end, the public eats the loss.

138
00:14:59,300 --> 00:15:05,920
You pay for the lottery ticket. They cash the winnings. You'd think someone would have mentioned this arrangement before

139
00:15:05,920 --> 00:15:13,520
now, and someone has, many times, but the people who cash the winnings also fund the campaigns of the people who could change

140
00:15:13,520 --> 00:15:18,940
the arrangement, so the arrangement continues. The only things the N.I.H.

141
00:15:18,940 --> 00:15:27,180
*should* fund are things private companies *cannot* profitably fund: open pragmatic trial platforms, repurposing of generic

142
00:15:27,180 --> 00:15:35,780
drugs where no patent means no profit means no interest, and comparative effectiveness data from head-to-head trials revealing

143
00:15:35,780 --> 00:15:41,360
which drugs actually work best (pharma's nightmare, your potential salvation).

144
00:15:42,100 --> 00:15:47,760
Instead, the N.I.H. explicitly avoids them to "not crowd out industry."

145
00:15:48,710 --> 00:15:55,630
Translation: "We won't run the trials that would lower drug prices, because the people who set drug prices asked us nicely."

146
00:15:56,440 --> 00:16:03,140
They asked nicely and also spent one hundred and twenty-seven million dollars on lobbying, but the niceness was noted.

147
00:16:04,240 --> 00:16:07,880
The Patient Disconnect: Zero Correlation with Health Outcomes.

148
00:16:09,140 --> 00:16:17,180
Correlation between N.I.H. funding priorities and actual disease burden: zero point zero seven.

149
00:16:18,240 --> 00:16:25,380
That is not a typo. On a scale of zero (random) to one (perfect), the N.I.H.

150
00:16:25,460 --> 00:16:31,600
scores "basically a coin flip." A dartboard would allocate research funding more rationally.

151
00:16:31,880 --> 00:16:31,980
A drunk person throwing darts at a list of diseases would produce a funding allocation that correlates more closely with

152
00:16:31,980 --> 00:16:38,080
actual disease burden than the National Institutes of Health, which employs twenty thousand people and has been doing this

153
00:16:38,080 --> 00:16:43,540
for fifty years. The dartboard doesn't even need to be in the building.

154
00:16:44,820 --> 00:16:53,140
Patients want medicine that works, access to experimental therapies, trials they can join, and a cure before they die.

155
00:16:53,880 --> 00:16:59,580
The N.I.H. funds molecular mechanisms, research empires, and institutional overhead.

156
00:17:00,220 --> 00:17:04,369
These two lists do not overlap. They don't even live in the same neighborhood.

157
00:17:05,859 --> 00:17:13,420
Patients don't control the money. Committees do. And committees optimize for the continued existence of committees.

158
00:17:13,880 --> 00:17:18,160
This is true of all committees, everywhere, since the invention of committees.

159
00:17:19,220 --> 00:17:22,940
Nobody has ever convened a committee that recommended fewer committees.

160
00:17:23,520 --> 00:17:28,880
On Wishonia, we have a word for an organism that exists solely to produce copies of itself.

161
00:17:29,480 --> 00:17:36,120
The word is "virus." You call them "advisory panels.". The Track Record.

162
00:17:36,520 --> 00:17:43,140
Since nineteen seventy, the N.I.H. has spent over one point one trillion dollars (inflation-adjusted).

163
00:17:43,440 --> 00:17:50,260
Diseases eradicated: Zero. This isn't because eradicating disease is impossible.

164
00:17:50,520 --> 00:17:54,520
The W.H.O. eradicated smallpox for three hundred million dollars.

165
00:17:55,340 --> 00:18:03,580
Jonas Salk developed the polio vaccine in a university lab and gave it away for free, which is why nobody at the N.I.H.

166
00:18:03,600 --> 00:18:10,480
talks about him at parties because he makes everyone look bad by comparison and also he's dead, which is the thing they

167
00:18:10,480 --> 00:18:18,240
were supposed to be fixing. Veterinarians have eradicated multiple animal diseases on budgets the N.I.H.

168
00:18:18,240 --> 00:18:23,520
would consider a rounding error. Pigs have better healthcare outcomes than you do.

169
00:18:24,040 --> 00:18:31,640
Pigs. Animals you lock in metal crates too small to turn around in, standing in their own waste, whose tails you amputate

170
00:18:31,640 --> 00:18:38,640
without anesthesia because the confinement drives them insane enough to eat each other (they're smarter than your dogs).

171
00:18:39,400 --> 00:18:43,780
Your veterinarians eradicated hog cholera in nineteen seventy-eight.

172
00:18:44,420 --> 00:18:50,580
The pigs couldn't turn around. You won't. What Would Actually Work.

173
00:18:51,420 --> 00:18:57,580
The U.K. RECOVERY trial already proved it. Five hundred dollars per patient instead of forty-one thousand dollars.

174
00:18:58,560 --> 00:19:03,680
First life-saving result in under one hundred days. Over one million lives saved.

175
00:19:04,180 --> 00:19:09,260
The blueprint exists. It is not theoretical. It happened. In England.

176
00:19:09,740 --> 00:19:13,860
During a pandemic. While Americans were arguing about horse dewormer.

177
00:19:14,580 --> 00:19:18,320
The evidence is right there. It's not hiding. It's waving at you.

178
00:19:18,780 --> 00:19:24,780
It has been waving at you for years. The recipe is simple enough that I can state it in four sentences.

179
00:19:25,920 --> 00:19:30,480
Pay for results: treatment equals payment, no treatment equals no payment.

180
00:19:31,480 --> 00:19:39,560
Publish everything, including failures, especially failures (because over fifty percent of trial results are never published,

181
00:19:40,060 --> 00:19:46,960
which means researchers unknowingly repeat experiments that already failed, which is how your species wastes an estimated

182
00:19:46,960 --> 00:19:54,380
eighty-five percent of its two hundred billion dollars annual research investment, and then passes the cost to patients

183
00:19:54,380 --> 00:20:02,380
as higher drug prices). Eliminate gatekeepers and let patients vote with participation, because the people who will die

184
00:20:02,380 --> 00:20:07,740
if the system fails are more motivated than the people who get tenure if the system continues.

185
00:20:08,840 --> 00:20:16,420
Automate administration with transparent systems instead of committees, because committees cannot be trusted (see above,

186
00:20:16,460 --> 00:20:22,680
and also see the entire history of committees, and also see the committee that was formed to investigate whether committees

187
00:20:22,680 --> 00:20:26,320
can be trusted, which concluded that committees are essential).
